00:43:47,590 --> 00:43:51,510
So here you get hate, but of course, you get support from the other side.

687
00:43:51,590 --> 00:43:54,510
That it's a good thing, there should be inclusion.

688
00:43:54,590 --> 00:43:55,700
Then they give their examples.

689
00:43:55,780 --> 00:44:01,700
Bangladesh also gives examples that financial inclusion is beneficial, family size is small, etc.

690
00:44:01,780 --> 00:44:03,557
Then all the solutions come.

691
00:44:03,637 --> 00:44:09,446
But if someone in our country talks like this, then it becomes anti-state, anti-national,

692
00:44:09,526 --> 00:44:09,826
etc.

693
00:44:09,906 --> 00:44:11,590
Of course, not in all cases,

694
00:44:11,670 --> 00:44:17,700
but in most cases as determined by what we see on social media and comments section.

695
00:44:17,780 --> 00:44:23,280
So this is absolutely true. We don't have to make the other side a building, but it's fun if the other side's building falls. Right?

696
00:44:23,360 --> 00:44:27,280
Because building a building requires hard work, and destroying it requires less hard work.

697
00:44:27,360 --> 00:44:34,280
Building a country requires hard work, and destroying it requires less hard work.

698
00:44:34,360 --> 00:44:40,280
We like to see others in pain, and we enjoy it.

699
00:44:40,360 --> 00:44:44,280
But if we think of it as a humanist level, then we should think that it is also a human being.

700
00:44:44,360 --> 00:44:51,360
It has basic rights, it should have freedom, it should have financial inclusion, it has children.

701
00:44:51,470 --> 00:44:55,340
They want health care, water, basic rights,

702
00:44:55,420 --> 00:45:02,880
but we don't go that way because if everyone starts thinking like this,

703
00:45:02,960 --> 00:45:06,190
the world can become very peaceful.

704
00:45:06,270 --> 00:45:12,190
And of course it is not the case and that is primarily because we enjoy other people's pain.

705
00:45:12,270 --> 00:45:15,190
So we need to have that rational voice that is somewhere else.

706
00:45:15,270 --> 00:45:17,190
It's the same like Pakistan.

707
00:45:17,270 --> 00:45:18,660
So well, the stupidity has no bias.

708
00:45:18,740 --> 00:45:24,660
There's no bounds. I hope we overcome this and move on from this.

709
00:45:24,740 --> 00:45:30,660
We don't think pragmatically. We don't think outside the box.

710
00:45:30,740 --> 00:45:34,660
As a third person, we don't take things as a third person.

711
00:45:34,740 --> 00:45:38,660
Whatever I believe in could be wrong as well. Right?

712
00:45:38,740 --> 00:45:44,740
So if I have a nationalistic belief, an ideological belief,

713
00:45:45,540 --> 00:45:55,337
Or if I give him a room where I am manipulated or my books are manipulated or I have been taught or explained,

714
00:45:55,417 --> 00:45:58,260
there can be some answer to that.

715
00:45:58,340 --> 00:46:03,260
There can be a mistake. So wherever you feel that room is there, then you look at things pragmatically.

716
00:46:03,340 --> 00:46:10,942
When you say that I believe in this thing and everything is this and we are the best in the world,

717
00:46:11,022 --> 00:46:12,700
then there is no room left.

718
00:46:12,780 --> 00:46:16,200
So you don't leave any room for improvement.

719
00:46:16,280 --> 00:46:18,200
You only look for other people's shortcomings.

720
00:46:18,280 --> 00:46:22,700
Then there are those things that you want to see in your country, but you are guilty.

721
00:46:22,780 --> 00:46:26,743
Deep down, if I were to say something to Dhruv Rathee, for example,

722
00:46:26,823 --> 00:46:29,975
if someone is in India and is not a Dhruv Rathee fan,

723
00:46:30,055 --> 00:46:32,200
he would understand that he is right.

724
00:46:32,280 --> 00:46:39,280
But he is so bound by his beliefs, his nationalistic or any ideological belief.

725
00:46:39,660 --> 00:46:49,140
And there is no courage to accept that what I have been believing for the past twenty years was wrong or my version or viewpoint was altered.

726
00:46:49,220 --> 00:46:51,140
And this is a new viewpoint.

727
00:46:51,220 --> 00:46:56,140
So as long as you keep yourself open to learning, you keep learning things.

728
00:46:56,220 --> 00:47:00,140
There are countless things I used to believe in.

729
00:47:00,220 --> 00:47:05,980
Politically, from a point of view of nationalism, there were many things.

730
00:47:06,060 --> 00:47:07,220
Obviously, this is how human evolution is.

731
00:47:07,300 --> 00:47:11,220
But that happens only when you keep your mouth shut.

732
00:47:11,300 --> 00:47:13,220
That yes, anything can happen.

733
00:47:13,300 --> 00:47:17,220
But when you shut yourself up, then you will never improve.

734
00:47:17,300 --> 00:47:22,220
So I think that deep down people can see that people are not that stupid.

735
00:47:22,300 --> 00:47:25,729
But because they don't have the courage to come and accept that yes,

736
00:47:25,809 --> 00:47:29,583
the thing I have been believing for 15 years or the party I used to follow,

737
00:47:29,663 --> 00:47:31,220
maybe it was not right for me.

738
00:47:31,300 --> 00:47:35,260
He did not make good decisions and I am thinking pragmatically and looking at things.

739
00:47:35,340 --> 00:47:37,260
and I think I was wrong.

740
00:47:37,340 --> 00:47:39,348
There's no shame in being wrong.

741
00:47:39,428 --> 00:47:47,260
There's no shame in accepting that you were wrong or you did something wrong or you believed something that was not true.

742
00:47:47,340 --> 00:47:51,260
It's about courage and your growth is in that.

743
00:47:51,340 --> 00:47:55,096
So I believe that deep down they understand that yes,

744
00:47:55,176 --> 00:47:59,260
Dhruv is not that wrong but he is so strong in his beliefs.

745
00:47:59,340 --> 00:48:02,340
Same goes for Pakistan. I'm sure people are talking about the Muslim world.

746
00:48:02,840 --> 00:48:06,680
They would understand, they would hit, they would ring the bell.

747
00:48:06,760 --> 00:48:10,662
But they would say, no, the propaganda that I am a victim of,

748
00:48:10,742 --> 00:48:14,480
how can I believe that I was wrong for twenty years?

749
00:48:14,560 --> 00:48:17,480
So my ego will be hurt that how was I wrong?

750
00:48:17,560 --> 00:48:19,060
So I am not wrong at all.

751
00:48:19,140 --> 00:48:24,980
So many times, I am pretty sure people understand things, but they are forced by their ego.

752
00:48:25,060 --> 00:48:28,140
And this ego itself takes you into life eventually.

753
00:48:28,220 --> 00:48:30,090
So always be humble, sit on the ground.

754
00:48:30,170 --> 00:48:33,410
Keep learning and keep learning.

755
00:48:33,490 --> 00:48:36,147
If you can learn something,

756
00:48:36,227 --> 00:48:43,010
don't think that you have to learn something because you are older.

757
00:48:43,090 --> 00:48:46,790
Keep yourself open and you will grow.

758
00:48:46,870 --> 00:48:54,310
Otherwise, you will not be able to reach the shore.

759
00:48:54,390 --> 00:48:58,100
Take care of yourself and please open your eyes and ears.

760
00:48:58,180 --> 00:49:00,100
and be very very pragmatic in life.

761
00:49:00,180 --> 00:49:02,100
It'll only do you good.

762
00:49:02,180 --> 00:49:05,100
And there's no harm in proving yourself wrong,

763
00:49:05,180 --> 00:49:09,100
or believing yourself that I was wrong somewhere.

764
00:49:09,180 --> 00:49:11,100
There's nothing wrong. There's only courage in that.

765
00:49:11,180 --> 00:49:13,100
So I hope you find that courage. Thank you.

766
00:49:13,180 --> 00:49:16,100
Alright, last question. Preserving ethnic culture.

767
00:49:16,180 --> 00:49:18,100
Hi, Junaid Bhai. Hope you're doing well.

768
00:49:18,180 --> 00:49:23,180
I'm writing to you for the second time. I'm 28 and settled in one of the European countries.

769
00:49:24,390 --> 00:49:26,283
Global World thanks to social media.

770
00:49:26,363 --> 00:49:30,764
I've been hearing a lot of voices coming from popular left of Pakistan,

771
00:49:30,844 --> 00:49:34,760
which says that we have forgotten our culture and our ethnic identity.

772
00:49:35,680 --> 00:49:37,215
By giving you my example,

773
00:49:37,295 --> 00:49:42,219
I don't know how could I have saved mine if you considered mine to be lost.

774
00:49:42,299 --> 00:49:43,329
Background.

775
00:49:43,409 --> 00:49:45,863
I was born and raised in South Punjab.

776
00:49:45,943 --> 00:49:47,284
My last name is Wasir.

777
00:49:47,364 --> 00:49:51,190
My parents spoke Punjabi at home when I was coming of age.

778
00:49:51,660 --> 00:49:52,717
I understand it,

779
00:49:52,797 --> 00:49:58,420
but I can't speak it and neither can I speak it in a dialect they speak it in.

780
00:49:58,500 --> 00:50:01,100
I speak Urdu like any other Pakistani.

781
00:50:01,180 --> 00:50:06,140
I grew up watching a mix of stuff on TV which involved Bollywood, Hollywood, etc.

782
00:50:06,220 --> 00:50:10,900
I ate the usual food we all eat like biryani or gosht with naan and dahi.

783
00:50:10,980 --> 00:50:14,620
I moved abroad for studies and job and now I am well settled.

784
00:50:14,700 --> 00:50:17,300
I try to infuse the stuff that I did back home.

785
00:50:17,410 --> 00:50:26,330
Here in the West to the best I can like eat the same food and speak the same language with friends and family but what else could I have done?

786
00:50:26,410 --> 00:50:31,330
How is it a blame that our generation faces that we forgot our culture?

787
00:50:31,410 --> 00:50:37,330
I am a Punjabi who was born and raised in Pakistan and I don't know anything that I could have done to carry on this legacy.

788
00:50:37,410 --> 00:50:40,504
Please explain to me what is the culture that I let go?

789
00:50:40,584 --> 00:50:44,980
Would love your insights on this issue and how can an individual carry the issue?

790
00:50:45,060 --> 00:50:46,980
carry their culture and keep it alive. Regards.

791
00:50:47,060 --> 00:50:51,900
Alright, so this is a question from a Pakistani brother from South Punjab.

792
00:50:51,980 --> 00:50:56,740
He lives in Europe. He doesn't know Punjabi.

793
00:50:56,820 --> 00:51:02,200
And preserving, what they say is that we have left our ethnic culture.

794
00:51:02,280 --> 00:51:06,620
One, I don't know how to differentiate it.

795
00:51:06,700 --> 00:51:09,700
I can't give an expert opinion on this.

796
00:51:09,780 --> 00:51:11,500
I can give you my example.

797
00:51:11,580 --> 00:51:13,000
Last week I found out I'm not a-

798
00:51:13,080 --> 00:51:14,272
I'm not a Punjabi.

799
00:51:14,352 --> 00:51:15,843
I'll tell you why.

800
00:51:15,923 --> 00:51:23,120
So basically, we knew all our lives that my grandparents, both sides, were from Kashmir.

801
00:51:23,200 --> 00:51:23,500
Okay.

802
00:51:23,580 --> 00:51:28,623
And there was a famine in Kashmir, I think in the 1920s, 1930s.

803
00:51:28,703 --> 00:51:34,656
So they came down from the mountains and the first settlement they got was Punjab.

804
00:51:34,736 --> 00:51:39,830
So when they lived in Punjab, then the culture of Punjab was adopted.

805
00:51:39,910 --> 00:51:41,630
All of them speak Punjabi, so they also learned.

806
00:51:41,710 --> 00:51:46,930
For example, I know a lot of Urdu-speaking families who speak better Punjabi than me.

807
00:51:47,010 --> 00:51:48,270
Like, they speak very well.

808
00:51:48,350 --> 00:51:50,270
Because they were part of that culture.

809
00:51:50,350 --> 00:51:52,270
And there, they were helpless.

810
00:51:52,350 --> 00:51:56,030
So my prefix is Khwaja.

811
00:51:56,110 --> 00:51:58,870
I don't use it, but my family uses it.

812
00:51:58,950 --> 00:52:00,470
My father used it.

813
00:52:00,550 --> 00:52:02,470
It didn't carry on until now.

814
00:52:02,550 --> 00:52:05,870
It's not documented. It's clearly not with Hashim, my son.

815
00:52:05,950 --> 00:52:07,060
But my cousins.

816
00:52:07,140 --> 00:52:11,060
My uncle's son has kept his name.

817
00:52:11,140 --> 00:52:16,060
Now I don't know what was the reason, whether my mother didn't like it or what it was.

818
00:52:16,140 --> 00:52:20,060
Anyway, so I was with my cousin.

819
00:52:20,140 --> 00:52:23,060
Unfortunately, he's diagnosed with cancer very last stage.

820
00:52:23,140 --> 00:52:29,060
So I don't know how much time he has. He's my uncle's son. He's like my brother.

821
00:52:29,140 --> 00:52:32,140
We spent a great amount of time. We live together.

822
00:52:33,800 --> 00:52:36,320
Great memories. Pray for him.

823
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:39,320
So he likes reminiscing about the past.

824
00:52:39,400 --> 00:52:42,440
He's been one of those guys who always lives in nostalgia.

825
00:52:42,520 --> 00:52:47,160
So that's when we realized that we're not Punjabis.

826
00:52:47,240 --> 00:52:55,200
But we grew up around like my grandparents grew up around them and then they picked up the language.

827
00:52:55,280 --> 00:52:57,760
And then we migrated here.

828
00:52:57,840 --> 00:52:59,780
Okay, so at home.

829
00:52:59,860 --> 00:53:06,460
Punjabi say Ami-Abu, Nana-Nani, Mamu-Mamu, but they didn't teach the kids.

830
00:53:06,540 --> 00:53:08,940
Neither did they encourage the kids to speak.

831
00:53:09,020 --> 00:53:15,140
Now if you add Karachi's ethnic violence of the 1990s, it was very anti-Punjab.

832
00:53:15,220 --> 00:53:19,980
It was considered a bad sentiment. Punjabis were not considered good.

833
00:53:20,060 --> 00:53:21,980
And now that we have grown up, we understand why.

834
00:53:22,060 --> 00:53:27,010
Because if every one of your grievances from three different areas.

835
00:53:27,090 --> 00:53:35,010
But then my Punjabi activist friends have counter arguments.

836
00:53:35,090 --> 00:53:39,010
These things have no solution. It's all about what he said and what she said.

837
00:53:39,090 --> 00:53:42,949
Anyway, so that didn't pass on to me.

838
00:53:43,029 --> 00:53:51,010
And I used to feel that the language will be there, but at least it ended on my side.

839
00:53:51,090 --> 00:53:54,090
And now it's absolutely over because my son speaks perfect.

840
00:53:54,690 --> 00:53:57,090
polite British version of English.

841
00:53:57,170 --> 00:54:01,170
And he says, yes, please, when you ask him, do you want chicken and chips?

842
00:54:02,850 --> 00:54:05,170
So that's a die down.

843
00:54:05,250 --> 00:54:11,490
So one thing is natural evolution, which is inevitable because of constant migrations.

844
00:54:11,570 --> 00:54:12,290
Right?

845
00:54:12,370 --> 00:54:16,530
Punjab came down from Kashmir, Karachi came from Punjab, then we took Urdu.

846
00:54:16,610 --> 00:54:19,890
From here, the next generation, I mean, the generation did not stop.

847
00:54:19,970 --> 00:54:22,450
Our two generations did not stop at one place.

848
00:54:22,580 --> 00:54:23,980
At least as much as I remember.

849
00:54:24,060 --> 00:54:25,740
Okay? They're just moving.

850
00:54:25,820 --> 00:54:28,220
So in movement, there is erosion.

851
00:54:28,300 --> 00:54:30,100
That erosion is of culture.

852
00:54:30,180 --> 00:54:31,540
It's of food.

853
00:54:31,620 --> 00:54:34,660
Now, I'm pretty, I don't eat saag. I don't like saag.

854
00:54:34,740 --> 00:54:36,420
Punjabis die for saag.

855
00:54:36,500 --> 00:54:37,980
I don't eat it.

856
00:54:38,060 --> 00:54:42,220
Ammi makes it every summer, every winter, sorry, summer, I'm saying.

857
00:54:42,300 --> 00:54:43,480
But she eats it herself.

858
00:54:45,640 --> 00:54:48,900
But like, culture keeps eroding.

859
00:54:48,980 --> 00:54:57,560
So this is an individual journey of culture that erodes due to intercultural marriages or migration.

860
00:54:57,640 --> 00:55:00,552
But the national level erosion that is done,

861
00:55:00,632 --> 00:55:06,280
the thoughtful crime that has been committed in our country, that has been very bad.

862
00:55:06,360 --> 00:55:11,080
And I think there is no justification for that.

863
00:55:11,470 --> 00:55:14,939
Our country was based on many ideas.

864
00:55:15,019 --> 00:55:20,970
One of them was Pan-Islamism, which was later put aside as a fort of Islam.

865
00:55:21,050 --> 00:55:22,970
I don't know how it became a fort, but it is.

866
00:55:23,050 --> 00:55:31,920
Because Islamization was so important that any cultural diversity, any language,

867
00:55:32,000 --> 00:55:34,970
any folk music stands nothing.

868
00:55:35,050 --> 00:55:38,850
Because the greater plan was that they were so toxic.

869
00:55:38,930 --> 00:55:40,850
They are filled with toxic.

870
00:55:40,930 --> 00:55:46,350
There is a famous writer of Pakistan, he used to write Urdu satire.

871
00:55:46,430 --> 00:55:50,850
He has a famous joke that he went to Saudi Arabia on a trip with a delegation.

872
00:55:50,930 --> 00:55:55,350
The Saudis said that we are Muslims but you have gone crazy after reading the Kalima.

873
00:55:55,430 --> 00:55:59,350
You have become too much Muslim. We are not that much Muslim.

874
00:55:59,430 --> 00:56:01,350
So it's a South Asian problem, right?

875
00:56:01,430 --> 00:56:04,380
Emotional people and just want to run ahead of others.

876
00:56:04,780 --> 00:56:08,755
So, because we needed the hour at some point,

877
00:56:08,835 --> 00:56:15,823
the Islamization of the state and the mindset of the people was the same,

878
00:56:15,903 --> 00:56:19,340
so we didn't have to give anything up.

879
00:56:19,420 --> 00:56:25,220
So, by design, things were destroyed.

880
00:56:25,300 --> 00:56:31,190
They made the Punjabi language a village language or a driver-labourer language.

881
00:56:31,270 --> 00:56:35,770
So your kids don't speak at all. They don't even speak when they come.

882
00:56:36,140 --> 00:56:40,923
But there are some artists who keep their representation alive.

883
00:56:41,003 --> 00:56:43,690
They speak, they talk in interviews.

884
00:56:43,770 --> 00:56:45,690
But that is a very small number.

885
00:56:45,770 --> 00:56:48,964
So our history is like our country's history.

886
00:56:49,044 --> 00:56:53,690
It starts from 712 AD when Muhammad bin Qasim came to this region.

887
00:56:53,770 --> 00:56:57,770
We don't talk about the great civilizations that once lived here.

888
00:56:59,110 --> 00:57:07,530
Brian Carson's podcast with Joe Rogan, that in the Mohenjo-Daro, skeletons were found.

889
00:57:07,610 --> 00:57:09,637
I don't know if it's true or not,

890
00:57:09,717 --> 00:57:13,729
but he said it and there are pictures that skeletons were found,

891
00:57:13,809 --> 00:57:17,310
people are holding hands and it doesn't look like an act.

892
00:57:17,390 --> 00:57:22,030
For example, if there was a flood, then the bones would be scattered around.

893
00:57:22,110 --> 00:57:25,350
Or if there was a meteor.

894
00:57:25,430 --> 00:57:29,350
There's been a nuclear war, so the bones should go far away.

895
00:57:29,430 --> 00:57:34,350
But there are people holding hands and it only happens when there's a nuclear war.

896
00:57:34,430 --> 00:57:39,350
Right? So, there's an estimation that something like this has happened here.

897
00:57:39,430 --> 00:57:45,350
There's been a calamity. Anyway, for the point being, that place is like six hours from me.

898
00:57:45,430 --> 00:57:51,350
I've never been there. Neither have we been incited there, nor have we been excited there.

899
00:57:51,430 --> 00:57:52,750
Nor have we been able to go on our school trip there.

900
00:57:52,830 --> 00:57:55,590
We don't know anything about them.

901
00:57:55,670 --> 00:57:58,310
The British are telling us. We didn't even look for the British.

902
00:57:58,390 --> 00:58:01,670
They were exploring gas and we were like, what is this?

903
00:58:01,750 --> 00:58:06,990
I was the only society found so far, correct me if I'm wrong, where there was no weapon.

904
00:58:07,070 --> 00:58:11,670
It was such a modern society that they had only availed peace.

905
00:58:11,750 --> 00:58:14,670
They didn't fight. There was no weapon.

906
00:58:14,750 --> 00:58:19,430
And we don't know about it. So our history is completely eroded.

907
00:58:19,580 --> 00:58:23,500
So when history is eroded, how will you find cultural traces?

908
00:58:23,580 --> 00:58:25,500
Then language, then our cuisines.

909
00:58:25,580 --> 00:58:31,500
Now, you live in the Middle East, you must have seen that you get South Indian cuisine.

910
00:58:31,580 --> 00:58:34,500
Indian cuisine, there's no such thing as an Indian cuisine.

911
00:58:34,580 --> 00:58:36,500
You find regional cuisine.

912
00:58:36,580 --> 00:58:41,500
You'll get Sindhi cuisine, South Indian cuisine, Bengali cuisine, right?

913
00:58:41,580 --> 00:58:44,580
Let's celebrate. What's a Punjabi cuisine?

914
00:58:44,710 --> 00:58:54,390
You can't have a Dumpakht, Balochi Tikka, or a Charsi Karahi, Charsi Tikka, or KP.

915
00:58:54,470 --> 00:58:58,630
So there's no celebration like this, regional wise.

916
00:58:58,710 --> 00:59:01,350
And whatever it is, it's a national Pakistani restaurant.

917
00:59:01,430 --> 00:59:03,830
Pakistani restaurants you will find all over the world.

918
00:59:03,910 --> 00:59:07,230
You will find Sindhi Biryani, Karahi, everything.

919
00:59:07,310 --> 00:59:09,030
